The  utility  is used to list and manipulate PaX flags associated
with an ELF program.  The PaX flags signify  to  the  loader  the
privilege  protections  to be applied to mapped memory pages, and
fuller explanations of the specific protections can be  found  in
the  manpage.   If  option is specified, all PaX flags (including
reserved bits) are cleared.  Otherwise, each flag can be prefixed
either with a or a sign to add or remove the flag,  respectively.
The  following  flags  are available: Explicitly disable PaX ASLR
(Address Space Layout Randomization) for  Explicitly  enable  PaX
ASLR  for  Explicitly disable PaX Segvguard for Explicitly enable
PaX Segvguard for Explicitly disable  PaX  MPROTECT  restrictions
for  Explicitly  enable PaX MPROTECT restrictions for To view ex‐
isting flags on a file, execute without any flags.   The  utility
first appeared in The utility is modeled after a tool of the same
name  available for Linux from the PaX project.  The utility cur‐
rently uses sections to mark executables as having PaX flags  en‐
abled.  This will be done using in the future so that we can con‐
trol who does the marking and not altering the binary file signa‐
ture.  (Note this also means that at present any flags set do not
survive binary file upgrades.)
